   "source": [
    "1) Explain the basic components of a digital image and how it is represented in a \n",
    "computer. State the differences between grayscale and color images?\n",
    "Ans:-\n",
    "Basic Components of a Digital Image \n",
    "A digital image is an electronic representation of a visual scene or object that can be \n",
    "stored and processed by a computer. \n",
    "It is made up of small elements called pixels (picture elements). Each pixel represents \n",
    "a tiny part of the image and holds information about its intensity and color. \n",
    "The three main components of a digital image are: \n",
    "1. Pixels: \n",
    "The smallest unit of a digital image. \n",
    "Each pixel has an intensity or color value that represents a small portion \n",
    "of the image. \n",
    "The higher the number of pixels (resolution), the clearer the image. \n",
    "2. Resolution: \n",
    "It defines the number of pixels in the image (width × height). \n",
    "Example: 1920 × 1080 resolution means the image has 2,073,600 pixels. \n",
    "Higher resolution means more detail and better image quality. \n",
    "3. Intensity (Brightness) Levels: \n",
    "The value that represents how bright or dark a pixel is. \n",
    "In an 8-bit image, intensity values range from 0 (black) to 255 (white). \n",
    "Representation of a Digital Image in a Computer \n",
    "A digital image is represented as a 2D matrix (array) of pixels. \n",
    "Each element in this matrix corresponds to one pixel and stores its intensity value. \n",
    "• For grayscale images: The matrix has one value per pixel (intensity). \n",
    "• For color images: The image is represented using three matrices (channels) — \n",
    "one each for Red, Green, and Blue (RGB) components. \n",
    "Example: \n",
    "• A grayscale image of 3×3 pixels → one 3×3 matrix. \n",
    "• A color image of 3×3 pixels → three 3×3 matrices (one for each color channel)."
   ]
  },
